      "A load board is an online marketplace where shippers post the loads they need moved and truckers post the trucks they have free. Both sides see the route, weight, vehicle type, and timing, then connect to agree on a price and move the freight. In India as of September 2026, load boards and app-based matching platforms have largely replaced the phone calls and newspaper columns that once did the same job. The concept is simple: one place where demand for trucks and supply of trucks can see each other.",
      "What a load board actually contains. On one side, a posted load carries origin, destination, cargo weight, goods type, required vehicle, pickup date, and often a budget range. On the other side, a posted truck (sometimes called posted capacity) carries the trucker's current location, vehicle type, available capacity in kilograms, and where they are heading next. When both sides match on corridor and timing, they negotiate or accept terms, then handle payment, documentation, and tracking like any other shipment.",
      "Where the idea came from. The load board concept predates the internet: North American dispatchers once ran the same flow on physical boards and phone trees. India's equivalent was never a board but a person, the local transport broker network operating out of transport nagars and dalas, who knew which truck was going where. Digital load boards formalise that broker's wall into a searchable list with filters, history, and payment records. See how this shifted in [digital freight matching in India](/blog/digital-freight-matching-india).",
      "The two basic formats. A list-style board shows every matching posting and leaves the calling, negotiating, and closing to you. An auto-matching platform does the ranking for you: it scores each pairing on route overlap, capacity fit, price alignment, departure timing, and the carrier's track record, then surfaces the strongest options first. Auto-matching matters when you do not have time to work a list of twenty truckers by phone, which is most working days for a small shipper.",
      "How matching works, step by step. Both parties are geocoded to coordinates, a road route is calculated between origin and destination, and the platform filters candidates by capacity in kilograms, vehicle type, and departure window. Surviving pairs are scored: same corridor and overlapping route earn the most, then capacity that actually fits the cargo, then price that falls inside the shipper's budget, then date fit, then the carrier's rating or credit history. The shipper sends a request, the trucker accepts, and chat, payment, and tracking switch on for that booking. Our own version of this flow is documented in [how LoadSaathi matching works](/blog/how-load-saathi-matching-works).",
      "For truckers, the main draw is the return load. India's Ministry of Road Transport and Highways estimates that over 35% of commercial truck-kilometres run empty, among the highest shares of any major trucking market. Every empty kilometre burns diesel and earns nothing. A load board turns a blind return leg into a posted opportunity: you see who needs a truck on the way back before you commit to the outbound trip. Practical tactics beyond boards are covered in our [guide to finding return loads in India](/blog/how-to-find-return-loads-india).",
      "For shippers, the draw is visibility and price benchmarks. Instead of calling three brokers for a truck on the Ranchi to Kolkata corridor, you open a board and see how many trucks are free this week, what vehicle types are available, and roughly what the corridor is paying. That visibility keeps you from overpaying in a tight week and from underpricing in a loose one. Before you book, cross-check the carrier's GST status using our [GST-verified transporters checklist](/blog/gst-verified-transporters) and consider [cargo insurance](/blog/cargo-insurance-guide-india) for the load itself.",
      "What load boards cost. Pricing models vary by platform: some charge truckers a monthly subscription for full access, some take a commission on each completed load, some are free for carriers and monetise shipper-side features, and broker-private boards pay per load posted. LoadSaathi-style marketplaces typically keep browsing free and charge only when a booking completes. Before paying anything, confirm three things: the board actually covers your corridors, the postings are verified rather than scraped, and payment is protected until delivery.",
      "Load board vs broker: both are legitimate, they just sit in different places. A broker takes ownership of the shipment, you hand over the freight and they arrange everything for a margin, which suits shippers who want one accountable party and no operational work. A load board or marketplace gives you visibility and tools; you choose the carrier, you set the terms, and you keep more of the rate. Many shippers run both, using a broker for their complex lanes and direct matching for routine corridor runs.",
